HARNESS ENFORCEMENT IS DISABLED IN THIS RELEASE — this is a known, deliberate state.

What this means
  The session-contract gate (.codex/hooks/session-contract-gate.cjs) returns early and
  enforces nothing while this file exists in .claude/, .codex/, or .pi/.
  Independent guards are unaffected and still run: force-push, destructive-git,
  commit, deploy, email, and GCS guards all remain active.

Why it is off
  With enforcement on, an unbound session (one with no contract in
  .agents/session-contracts/) is blocked from every mutating shell command —
  including `npm test`, `git commit`, and `mkdir`. A fresh clone therefore cannot
  run its own test suite. File edits were already opened up for exactly this reason
  (see the comment above unboundOrdinaryMutationAllowed), but the shell path was
  never given the same treatment.

  A guard nobody can work under is not enforcing anything, so it is off rather than
  silently blocking newcomers.

Known inconsistency
  AGENTS.md / CLAUDE.md / GEMINI.md still describe the contract requirement as if it
  were enforced. That text documents the intended design, not current runtime behavior.
  The Session Boundaries section carries a note pointing here.

Before removing this file
  Fix the shell path so ordinary non-destructive commands run unbound, then verify on
  a fresh clone that `npm test` and an ordinary commit succeed with enforcement on,
  and re-run cross-host regression tests. Removing it without that fix will block
  every new user.

